  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered thunderstorm coverage is observed over\neastern NC in a very unstable, very moist environment. A weak\nshortwave sitting over the region and deep shear of 20-30 knots\nis providing enough forcing and maintenance out along and ahead\nof the sea breeze for a risk of deep cores in thunderstorms.\nThis brings a risk of locally heavy rainfall, frequent\nlightning, and gusty winds with the strongest of storms. Threat\nof storms quickly wanes with a loss of daytime heating late this\nevening.\n\nHeat will be the name of the game over the next few days as the\nBermuda high centered off the coast will slide S'wards towards \nthe Gulf Coast States while a thermal trough remains over the \nPiedmont. This will keep low level flow generally SW to W over \nthe next several days. As a result, some weak WAA will allow low\nlevel thicknesses to increase closer to 1430m. Based off the \noffices climatological study this is forecast to result in highs\nin the low to mid 90s across the inland areas and upper 80s to \nlow 90s along the OBX and coast through much of the week before \na front impacts the region resulting in cooler temperatures late\nweek into the weekend. \n\nHeat indices are peaking around 100-105 this afternoon, but are\nexpected to climb to around 105-110 Tue through Thurs, and \npotentially lingering into Fri with heat headlines probably for \nmuch of the area. In fact, have issued a Heat Advisory for\nsouthern portions of the CWA with the afternoon update. Areas\nnorth could get there, but uncertainty is higher as MCS\ncurrently moving through the Ohio Valley may bring more cloud\ncover and lower Tds with any remnant outflows that move into the\nregion. Lows will be in the 70s which won't provide much relief\nfrom the heat in the coming days. We encourage anyone planning \non spending alot of time outdoors this week to take proper \nprecautions and stay hydrated.\n\nOtherwise, a typical summer time pattern will be in place \nthrough much the week. Near zonal flow aloft with a series of \nshortwave troughs pushing across the area will result climo \nprecip chances for the next several days with best chances \nthrough peak heating and seabreeze related for the most part but\nwill also depend on timing and exact location of individual \nshortwave troughs. The severe threat appears minimal for the \nnext couple of days with limited shear, though somewhat better \nshear develops toward the middle of the week. We do look to have\nincreased precipitation chances closer to Fri and into next \nweekend with the approach of an incoming cold front.",
